{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"edits\":[{\"path\":\"formation/text\",\"value_json\":\"\\\"The current graph 学 is arranged ⿳⺍冖子: ⺍ and 冖 form an upper assembly above 子. This visible split does not settle how traditional 學 was first constructed. One account interprets oracle-bone forms as 𦥑 (two hands) with 五 and 六, while allowing that 爻 may supply a sound cue in some forms. Separate proposals describe phonosemantic forms with 爻 as sound and 冖 as the other element; a rare, unidentified element read jù and depicted as hands or claws; and a construction grouping two 臼 forms with 冖 and a following unidentified printed graphic unit to which the source assigns the sound role. That proposed role belongs to the graphic after 冖, not to either 臼 or to the whole 学 construction. The two 臼 shapes may be reduced to an abbreviated form; the exact graphic identity of that form is unresolved. In the two-臼 proposal, the graphic after 冖 resembles the earlier graph labeled jù; whether they are the same visible graph remains unresolved. The rare jù account and the two-臼 proposal remain distinct. These are competing interpretations, not a settled reconstruction. One source also proposes learning and emulation as 學’s original meaning, but the proposal is unsettled. The modern simplified shape does not establish the ancient structure.\\\"\"},{\"path\":\"uncertainties/0/text\",\"value_json\":\"\\\"The early construction accounts remain distinct. One interprets some forms as 𦥑 (two hands) with tally-like elements 五 and 六, while allowing that 爻 may supply a sound cue in some forms. Other proposals describe phonosemantic forms with 爻 as sound and 冖 as the other element; a separate, unusual element read jù and depicted as hands or claws; and a distinct construction grouping two 臼 forms with 冖 and a following unidentified printed graphic unit to which the source assigns the sound role. That role is assigned to the graph after 冖, not to either 臼 or to the whole 学 construction. The two 臼 shapes may be reduced to an abbreviated-looking graph whose exact identity remains unresolved; a human-like shape is not established. In the two-臼 proposal, the graphic after 冖 resembles the earlier graph labeled jù; whether they are the same visible graph remains unresolved. The rare jù element is not identified as 臼, and its proposal remains distinct from the two-臼 account. These proposals do not establish one construction for every early form. The claim that 學’s original meaning was learning and emulation is a proposal, not an established conclusion.\\\"\"}]}"}}
